Chapter One — The Bridge

The morning came up pale and colorless over the water, the sun no more than a white coin pressed behind a wall of fog that had swallowed the far shore whole. Dusty stood at the mainland foot of the bridge with a canvas satchel across one shoulder and everything he owned inside it, and while he waited he counted.

Nine guards at the near gate, their torso plate burnished to a soft gray sheen in the flat light, spears standing taller than the men who carried them, shields slung across their backs like the shells of enormous beetles. Two more walked the roof of the gatehouse where the crenellations bit into the sky. The nearest man wore a chip out of his left greave that nobody had troubled to hammer out, and the raw metal inside the gouge still shone bright and unweathered, which made it this week's damage, which meant the Guard had been busy somewhere he could not see.

Behind him Aethres was waking and making a great deal of noise about it, cart wheels grinding over wet stone, a fishmonger crying a price into the damp, shutters banging open three streets back. Ahead of him there was only the bridge, a span of pale stone six carriages wide running out into all that white and vanishing before it ever reached the other side.

He had said his goodbye an hour ago in a narrow room with clean sheets and a window that let in honest light, which was still the part he could not get his mind to lie flat against. Clean sheets. Rinnie had been sitting up straighter than her body wanted, chin lifted, the reassuring smile already built and waiting for him by the time he turned in the doorway.

*Go on,* she had said. *You'll be late, and then they'll write it down.*

At the last moment she had pressed something into his palm, folded up in a scrap of waxed paper, and closed his fingers over it with both of her small cold hands. He had not looked at it yet. He could feel the shape of it now through the strap against his ribs, flat and light and weighing more than everything else in the bag together.

A horn sounded somewhere up the line, long and low across the water, and Dusty stepped onto the bridge.

The carriages came behind him and the carriages stopped.

He heard the whole thing happen twenty paces back without turning his head, because turning invited invitation, and invitation from that class of person always carried a price that never appeared on the receipt. Hooves cut short, a driver swearing at his team, and then the silence of a great many well-dressed people arriving at the discovery that the rule included them.

"—any conception whatsoever of whose seal that is—"

"The bridge is walked alone, my lord." The guard's voice came flat as a dropped coin, bored with itself, a man saying a line he had already said forty times since dawn and would say sixty more before the light went. "Trunks travel with the porters. Attendants remain in Aethres. You are welcome to walk."

There was a pause with a great deal of expensive breathing in it, and then there were footsteps.

Dusty allowed himself one glance sideways as the young man overtook him, because you always looked, because knowing which shapes in a room could hurt you was not curiosity but maintenance. Crimson doublet, gold thread braided at the cuffs and worth more than the building Dusty had slept in for the first fifteen years of his life. The jaw clenched hard enough to stand the muscle out like a knot in wet rope. Hands opening and closing at his sides over and over, hunting for something to be angry at that would not cost him anything to be angry at.

The young lord looked at him as he passed, taking in the patched coat and the boots resoled twice and the satchel with everything in it, and whatever conclusion he arrived at he arrived at quickly, and it improved his morning considerably. He went on up the span with his shoulders squared.

Dusty held his pace exactly where it had been. Being measured wrong had never cost him anything, and it had fed him more than once. He watched the crimson diminish into the white ahead and noted a long stride and no callus worth the name on the right palm, and then he let the man go out of his mind entirely.

The fog thinned at the center of the span. He had time to watch it happen, the white going gauzy and then gold and then tearing open all at once like a curtain hauled off a window, and the light that came through it fell on the water first and turned the whole lake to beaten silver from his boots out to the horizon.

Mythrandis stood in the middle of it.

The towers came first because the towers had been built to come first, three of them at the island's heart, pale as bone and taller than anything he had ever seen raised by hands. The low sun had them across their eastern faces and they threw it back so hard he had to narrow his eyes against the glare. Below them the island spilled outward in green, and it was not the exhausted green of the Greys where a weed in a gutter counted as an event but a deep, extravagant, impossible green, forest and garden and terraced hedge running down to a shoreline that glittered white because somebody had faced it in marble, for no reason he could name beyond the fact that they could. Gulls hung over the water in slow circles. Somewhere among the towers a bell was ringing the hour, and the sound came out across the lake thinned and sweetened by all that distance.

There was no smoke. That was the thing that stopped him. Fifty square miles of building and five thousand people living on it and not one column of chimney smoke standing anywhere in that sky.

Dusty stayed where he was a moment longer than he needed to, and not out of wonder. He was pricing it — the height of the walls, the rotation on the gate, the sight lines those three towers held over every road on the island, the swimming distance from the northern shore to the smudge of the far bank if a man ever had to make it.

Six weeks ago he had been trading two hours of hauling for the heel of a loaf and working out whether there was enough of it to split.

*Does this keep her safe.*

It did. He shifted the strap on his shoulder and walked.

She was waiting at the far gate, and she had not been standing there a moment before.

He was reasonably certain of it. He had swept the archway twice on the approach the way he swept every doorway he had ever walked through in his life, and it had held nothing but wet stone and two guards and a wedge of shadow. Now there was a woman in the middle of it with her hands folded at her waist, composed as a statue in a niche, giving the impression that she had been there since the first stone was laid and the rest of the world had simply failed to look.

Black dress, buttoned high and close at the throat. A white apron so clean it took the morning light and hurt to look at. The skirt was cut just above the knee over white stockings, and the black fabric above it had been tailored by someone with a great deal of talent and an impossible assignment, drawn taut across a figure it contained without ever managing to disguise. Black lace gloves ran to her wrists. Her heels were black and thin as nails and should not have been able to stand on cobblestone at all, let alone stand on it without sinking. Dark hair pinned up with not one strand loose. Her mouth was painted red and applied with the exactness of a signature at the bottom of a contract.

She was smiling, and the smile reached her eyes, and Dusty did not trust it for a moment — not because it was false, but precisely because it wasn't.

"Dusty Desert." It was not a question. "Welcome to Mythrandis. I am Mirelle. I will be showing you the island today."

"Thank you, ma'am." He inclined his head. "I was told someone would meet me. I thought it would be Researcher Velanthus."

"Researcher Velanthus intended to." Something crossed the corner of Mirelle's mouth, brief as a wingbeat. "She is in the east laboratory. She has been in the east laboratory since the day before yesterday. Someone will feed her at noon."

"That sounds right."

"It usually is." She turned, and her hand came out and lifted the satchel off his shoulder before he had finished deciding whether to allow it. "This will be in your room before you are."

The crimson doublet arrived at the gate behind them at speed.

"You." The finger came up. "My trunks are being manhandled by dockworkers. See that they are brought to my—"

Mirelle turned to face him and the smile did not alter by a hair.

The trunks were coming up the road behind him on a low cart, brass at the corners and big as coffins, three porters walking alongside and unhappy about the grade of the hill. Mirelle stepped off the path, took the strap handle of the nearest one in a single lace-gloved hand, and lifted it off the boards.

She did not brace for it. She did not shift her weight to receive it. The thin heels did not sink a hair into the ground.

She set it down on the cobbles beside the young lord's boots with a sound like a dropped headstone, straightened, and folded her hands again at her waist.

"All actions must be with Beauty, Elegance, and Dignity above all else," she said pleasantly. "Yours will be brought up, my lord. There is no need to raise your voice on a bridge. Sound carries a long way over water."

The silence that came after it had a texture to it, and it went on a little.

Dusty watched the young lord's face work through three separate expressions before it settled on the one belonging to a man who does not understand what has just happened to him but has correctly identified that something has. He made a small sound in his throat, and he went up the road without looking back, and the porters found several places to put their eyes that were not Mirelle.

Only Dusty had been watching her forearm through it, and only Dusty had seen that the lace across it never once pulled tight. Whatever that trunk weighed, her arm had not been told about it.

"Shall we?" said Mirelle.

"Yes, ma'am," said Dusty, and followed her in.

She walked him east through a morning that kept refusing to be believable.

The bridge district came first, guard quarters and workhouses and the long low buildings where the island's business got done out of sight of the students it was done for, smoke-free chimneys and swept yards and a smell of clean water and cut grass instead of the wet rot he had grown up breathing. Then the road opened out and the road was lined with trees, and the trees had been put in the ground by somebody who knew exactly how tall they would stand in a hundred years and had planted for the shade they would throw long after he was dead.

"North is where you will sleep," Mirelle said. "The dormitories. Single rooms, all of them, and the assignments are random, whatever you hear from students who cannot bear the idea that a thing was not arranged in their favor. It was not arranged at all." A pause, half a step long. "By anyone you will ever meet."

"Understood."

"Central is where your waking hours will go. Lecture halls, the libraries, the research wings. The towers hold the administration and the Headmaster at the top of them, and you will in all likelihood never see him. If you do, then something has gone extremely well or extremely badly, and you will know which without being told."

Marble statues stood along the courtyards with the morning coming down over their shoulders, and fountains ran in the middle of them, catching the light in white ropes and breaking it across the basins. Nobody was drinking from them and nobody was watching to see whether anyone would. Students crossed the paths in twos and threes, blazers in red and blue and dark green, black ties and ribboned collars and boots polished bright, girls laughing with their heads tipped back and their skirts swinging, a boy carrying six books that were plainly not his while their owner walked three paces ahead of him and did not turn around once.

Dusty catalogued all of it without any effort at all. Who walked in front, who walked half a step behind and to the left, whose laugh came a beat late because it was waiting for permission. It was the same arrangement he had watched work itself out in alleys over half a loaf of bread, and the only thing that had changed was the quality of the cloth it was wearing.

"Dining halls, seven of them, spread through the central district. Breakfast at seven, lunch at twelve, dinner at six." Mirelle's heels struck the stone in a rhythm as level as a metronome. "Tea is at four. Tea is not optional in the way you are about to assume it is optional."

"No, ma'am."

"Curfew is at ten. The Guard hold the island's edges and the catacomb entrances. There are a great many doors on this island that are locked, and every one of them is locked for a reason, and the reasons are considerably older than your family name." She said it as lightly as she had said everything else. "Students test this. It is traditional. I would rather you did not."

"I have no plans to."

"No." Mirelle glanced across at him once, and it was not a long look, but it went the whole way down. "No, I don't imagine you do."

Further east she brought him along a wall of glass that ran the length of a building longer than any street he had ever lived on. The air behind it was visibly warmer, the panes fogged at their lower corners, greenery crowding up against them in broad wet leaves and climbing vine, rows of low herb beds running back into a golden haze thick enough to swim in. Figures in white moved among the beds. A woman knelt at a cot beside a boy with a splinted arm, and her hands were glowing a soft buttery yellow that lit the underside of her jaw and the boy's sleeve where it was rolled, and as Dusty watched, the boy's shoulders came down out of his ears.

He stopped walking. He had not decided to.

"The Solarium," said Mirelle, who had stopped when he did. "Infirmary and growing houses both. You will rotate through it in your second term, and after that, if you are any good, they will stop rotating you and simply expect you." She let a beat go by. "Inside those doors there is no rank at all. Not house, not blood, not lottery. A duke's daughter and a fisherman's son bleed on adjacent tables and are seen in the order they arrived, and any student who forgets it is carried out of the building by us. Not by the Guard. By us."

"Who enforces it when the Maids aren't there?"

"There is no such condition," said Mirelle.

He looked at her, and she looked back at him entirely pleasantly, and behind the glass the yellow light went out as the woman took her hands away.

"Thank you," Dusty said. "That's the first thing anyone's told me today that I can use."

Something moved behind Mirelle's face, nothing so coarse as surprise, only a small internal recalibration filed somewhere he would never be permitted to read.

"South," she went on as they turned, "is faculty housing, and the Maid Corp quarters, and the docks. The docks are not for students. The forest along the southern shore is not forbidden, but it is not recommended, and I will tell you plainly that the difference between those two words matters a great deal less than you would like it to."

"May I ask you one thing?"

"You may ask me a great many things. I answer some of them."

"Where does the mail go out from?"

Mirelle stopped.

It was the only time all morning that her rhythm broke, and it lasted less than a heartbeat, and if he had been raised anywhere else he would not have caught it at all.

"The correspondence office. Central district, the western face, second floor." Her head tilted a fraction. "Everything is read, in and out, every letter without exception, including the ones with imperial seals on them. I tell every student that on the first day and most of them are outraged."

"I'm not."

"No." The red mouth curved. "You have nothing to hide and somebody to write to. That is a rarer arrangement here than you would believe. Second floor, western face. They open at eight."

Tea happened at four, and he watched it from the edge of a courtyard the way he had once watched a market from the mouth of an alley, reading the flow of it, learning where the money moved and who it moved toward.

He had his blazer by then, dark green and stiff with newness, and it sat well enough across his shoulders that he understood somebody had taken his measurements without his noticing them do it. Under the pavilions the maids went among the tables in black and white, pouring amber tea out of silver pots in long steaming arcs, and the low sun came slantwise through the pavilion columns and laid bars of gold across the linen. At one table a girl with a white shawl across her shoulders said something that made nine people laugh at the same time. At another, three lower-born students sat very straight and did not touch their cups until somebody else touched theirs first.

Nobody spoke to him. Two people looked at his tie and then at his boots and adjusted their route across the courtyard, and that suited him precisely, because he had never in his life wanted anything from a room except to be unremarkable inside it.

His room was on the third floor of the northern dormitory, second to last along the corridor, and it was two rooms, which he had not expected.

A washroom with a copper tub standing on clawed feet and water that came out of the tap warm, which he ran twice to be certain of. Then the room proper: a desk under the window, a chair, a wardrobe, and a bed with four pillows on it when one would have been generous and none was what he had grown up sleeping without. The satchel sat waiting on the desk exactly as promised, and he had not passed a single maid on the stairs coming up.

Dusty set his palm flat against the door and pushed it closed, and stood a while in the dark with his back against it, and then he went to work.

The window first. It opened, and it opened onto a ledge, and the ledge ran twelve feet to a downspout that would hold his weight for four seconds, which was three more than he would ever need out of it. Twenty feet from the ledge down to grass in the garden below, survivable if he took it rolling. The wardrobe was deep enough to stand inside. The desk would come across the doorway in under two seconds if he shifted it eight inches to the left, so he shifted it eight inches to the left and stood the chair where his hand would find it. Out in the corridor there was a board that spoke when it was stepped on, four paces from his threshold, and he walked it twice to be sure of where it lay.

None of it was fear. It was the same as testing a knot before you hung your weight off it. The body did not unlearn survival because somebody had finally given it clean sheets.

When it was finished he sat down on the edge of the bed in a dark green blazer worth more than his mother's funeral and took the waxed paper packet out of the satchel and opened it across his knee.

Inside was a flower. Pressed flat and dried through to translucence, a small five-petaled thing the pale blue of a winter sky with its veins standing dark against the light, and it had grown in a clay pot on the windowsill of a healer's house in Aethres, and it had a name, because Rinnie named all of them, and she had never once told him what this one was called.

He held it in the flat of his hand for a while.

Then, because it was habit and because the room was very quiet, he let the mana come up.

It rose the way it always had, without any rush to it and nothing like a flood, only the small steady wick of it catching somewhere beneath his sternum and running warm down the insides of his arms into his fingers. The glow bloomed across both palms and it lit nothing whatsoever. Black light does not illuminate. It only tells you where the hands are, a darkness that shone, hemmed all around its edges in a moving thread of silver that ran the lines of his fingers the way water runs a channel. Three separate hedge-healers in the Greys had refused to lay a hand on him over that color, and one of them had spat.

He warmed the air above the flower. Not the flower — you did not put mana into a dead thing, it passed straight through and came out the other side with nothing to show for the trip. Only the air over it, held at the temperature of a hand.

Eleven winters he had done exactly this, all night, every night, in rooms with no glass standing in the windows. A small fire and no well beneath it to drain. He had never been the strongest thing in any room he had ever stood in and it had never once mattered, because he had never needed to be the strongest.

He had only ever needed to not stop.

There was nobody in this room to keep warm.

Dusty sat with his hands burning black and silver in the dark a long moment, and then he closed them, and the light went out of the room, and he set the flower on the desk against the base of the lamp where it would be the first thing he saw in the morning.

He lay down on top of the blankets with his boots on and his back to the wall, facing the door.

Out over the lake, the towers rang the hour, and the sound came in through the glass thinned and sweetened by the distance the way it had that morning from the middle of the bridge.

He was asleep before the third bell, and he slept the way he had always slept, lightly, one layer down, the part of him that counted exits still awake and still counting.
